<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Cleartext HTTP to the host this app is being developed on, and to nothing
  else.

  WHY IT EXISTS. The level 2 facade run (packages/example-app/level-two)
  stands a throwaway Matrix homeserver and a matrix-nio counterparty up on
  the developer's own machine, on the loopback interface, and the app talks
  to both. There is no certificate to trust for a container that exists for
  ninety seconds, and there should not be: everything here is loopback
  traffic between two processes on one machine.

  WHY IT IS NOT usesCleartextTraffic="true". That placeholder is what React
  Native's own Gradle plugin sets, true for debug and false for release, and
  it is all or nothing per build type. A release APK is what CI builds and
  what scripts/run-probe-on-emulator.sh installs, so the level 2 run needs
  cleartext in a release build, and granting it globally there would let this
  app speak plaintext HTTP to anything on the internet. A network security
  config wins over that attribute for the domains it names, so the effect of
  this file is:

    release: cleartext to the three names below, denied everywhere else
             (previously: denied everywhere).
    debug:   cleartext to the three names below, denied everywhere else
             (previously: allowed everywhere), which is strictly tighter.
             Metro is reached at 10.0.2.2:8081 or localhost:8081, both of
             which are named here.

  10.0.2.2 is the standard Android emulator's alias for the host's loopback
  interface; 127.0.0.1 and localhost are what the same host looks like when
  adb reverse is in play. None of the three is routable off the machine.

  RULE FOR ANYONE ADDING A DOMAIN BELOW: a name here is a name this app may
  speak unencrypted HTTP to. Only add one that cannot leave the developer's
  own machine. A real product ships no such file.
-->
<network-security-config>
  <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false" />
  <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
    <domain includeSubdomains="false">10.0.2.2</domain>
    <domain includeSubdomains="false">127.0.0.1</domain>
    <domain includeSubdomains="false">localhost</domain>
  </domain-config>
</network-security-config>
